QEMU device models for the Raspberry Pi 3B port
===============================================

Patches against qemu v10.1.3, the version these boards are tested on.
They are here because parts of the Pi 3B are either unmodelled by QEMU
or modelled in a way that cannot exercise the guest driver: without
them the corresponding kernel code is written blind.

To apply and build:

	git clone --depth 1 --branch v10.1.3 \
		https://gitlab.com/qemu-project/qemu.git
	cd qemu && git am /path/to/tools/qemu-patches/*.patch
	mkdir build && cd build
	../configure --target-list=aarch64-softmmu --disable-docs \
		--disable-fuse --disable-jack
	ninja

Run the result by explicit path; do not install over a packaged qemu.
On macOS the binary is qemu-system-aarch64-unsigned, which runs fine
under TCG.

EVIDENCE TIERS.  Results in doc/raspi3b-completion-plan.ms are labelled
by what they were checked against, strongest first:

	hardware	a real Raspberry Pi 3B
	qemu		a device model QEMU already shipped
	qemu+patch	a device model in this directory

qemu+patch is the weakest of the three and is called out wherever it is
used.  Both halves are ours there, so a passing test can mean the model
implements the driver's bug.  Two rules keep that honest, and they are
worth keeping when adding to this series:

  - write the model from the hardware documentation or an existing
    Linux/9front driver, not from what the Inferno driver happens to
    emit, and say in the patch where it deviates;

  - make the model reject protocol violations rather than tolerate
    them.  QEMU's own usb-msd answering "Bad CBW size" is what caught
    the dropped bulk maxpkt in appl/cmd/usb/usbd.b; a permissive model
    would have hidden it.

THE PATCHES

0001 bcm2835_powermgt: model the watchdog countdown.

	Stock QEMU resets the machine the instant a guest sets RSTC's
	full-reset bit, and logs PM_WDOG as unimplemented.  The
	watchdog therefore cannot be armed, watched, fed or stopped,
	and os/inferno/raspi3b/pmbcm.c's arm-and-stop self-test
	reboot-loops against it.

	With the patch the counter ticks at 65536 Hz, counts only
	while RSTC selects full-reset, reads back the time remaining,
	and freezes where it stopped.  Linux's reboot path is
	unaffected: it arms with 10 ticks, about 150us.

0002 bcm2835_gpio: honour alt3 on the SD card pins.

	Stock QEMU recognises only alt0 (SDHOST) on GPIO 48-53.  The
	alt3 write that hands the card to the Arasan SDHCI block --
	what sdhcibcm.c does, and what the hardware requires -- is
	ignored, so the card stays wherever it was parented.  Since it
	is parented to SDHCI at reset, the SDHCI driver appears to
	work while its routing write does nothing, and a driver that
	omitted the write entirely would pass identically.

	With the patch alt3 selects SDHCI, so the routing is real: the
	card can be handed to SDHOST and taken back.

SELF-TESTS

Two kernel self-tests are compiled out by default, because each can
leave the board unusable if the hardware does not behave -- on a real
Pi as much as under an emulator -- and a boot-time check that can brick
its own boot is not worth having on by default.

	-DWDOGSELFTEST	pmbcm.c: arm the watchdog, watch it count
			down, stop it.  If the stop does not take, the
			machine reboots 8 seconds later, and again
			after that.

	-DSDROUTETEST	sdhcibcm.c: route the card to SDHOST and
			back.  If it cannot be taken back, the board
			has no SD card.

Build them by compiling the two files by hand with the define, then
relinking:

	cd os/inferno/raspi3b
	mk
	7c -wFV -DWDOGSELFTEST -DSDROUTETEST ... pmbcm.c sdhcibcm.c
	touch -t 203001010000 pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7
	mk

The touch is needed because mk rebuilds an object whose timestamp
merely ties with its source.

What they print, against patched qemu with a 16M raw SD image
(-drive file=sd.img,if=sd,format=raw):

	WDOG-PRESENT loaded and holding while disarmed
	WDOG-OK armed, counted down and stopped
	sdhci: alt0 released the card, alt3 took it back
	SDROUTE-OK
	SDHCI-OK

Against stock qemu the watchdog test reboot-loops and never reaches a
banner, and the routing test reports SDROUTE-FAIL.  The default kernel,
with neither define, boots clean on both.
